title: Choosing a whole number
mode: draw
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
parts: chip:my_mux
needs: my_mux
---- intro
Your **my_mux** chooses one bit. To choose between two **numbers**, `a1 a0` and `b1 b0`, use one multiplexer per bit, all listening to the same `s`:

- `s` is 0: `y1 y0` is `a1 a0`
- `s` is 1: `y1 y0` is `b1 b0`
---- task
Make `y1 y0` follow `a1 a0` when `s` is 0, and `b1 b0` when `s` is 1, with two **my_mux** chips.
---- start
input a0 0 0
input b0 0 60
input a1 0 150
input b1 0 210
input s 0 300
led y0 400 20
led y1 400 170
---- reference
input a0, b0, a1, b1, s
led y0, y1
y0 = (a0 & ~s) | (b0 & s)
y1 = (a1 & ~s) | (b1 & s)
---- answer
input a0 0 0
input b0 0 60
input a1 0 150
input b1 0 210
input s 0 300
chip m0 my_mux 200 10
chip m1 my_mux 200 160
led y0 400 20
led y1 400 170
a0 -> m0.a
b0 -> m0.b
s -> m0.s
m0.y -> y0
a1 -> m1.a
b1 -> m1.b
s -> m1.s
m1.y -> y1
---- hint1
One my_mux for bit 0 (`a0`, `b0`) and one for bit 1 (`a1`, `b1`).
---- hint2
Both multiplexers get the same `s`. Their outputs go to `y0` and `y1`.
---- solution
Two my_mux chips side by side, one per bit, sharing `s`. Flip `s` and both bits switch at once.
---- done
A processor chooses 32 or 64 bits at a time like this: one multiplexer per bit, one select wire for all of them.
